* IDE
* A development environment on the screen library: a tree of the account's files and their records, an editor
* with syntax colour and line numbers (one tab for each record that is open), a table of the compiler's errors
* or of search results, a menu bar and a status line.
* Copyright (c) 2026, SD Core for Linux.  This program is free software; you can redistribute
* it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 18 Oct 26 dm  Part B of the 140x42 plan: Ctrl-E, and File / Edit in SED, hand the record to SED and take it back when SED saves it (do.sed;
*               the editor's passkeys leave Ctrl-E to the IDE, so End is the key for the end of a row here).  Also the owner's three reports:
*               the cursor is put back after the status line (and the line is written only when it changes), a record or a tab is drawn
*               by its own widgets (redraw.editor, redraw.tree, focus.to; SCREEN FOCUS moves a pointer and draws nothing), and the editor
*               has a horizontal scroll bar (hbar).
* 18 Oct 26 dm  Terminal 140 by 42 (plan-terminal-140x42.md, phase 3).  The IDE is laid out for at least 140 by 42 (it was 60 by 16) and
*               refuses a smaller window at the start with the library's one message ($sl_need, 11197; 11198 is no longer used).
*               A window of more than that is still used whole.  A window made smaller after the start shows the upper left corner
*               of the layout (the layout stays 140 by 42, the library cuts what falls outside the window), and when the editor's
*               or the tree's cursor is off the screen $sl_key takes the keyboard (the cursor lock, $sl_guardmode) until the window
*               is made bigger; Ctrl-Q, y there makes $sl_key return guardquit, and the IDE leaves without saving.
* 18 Oct 26 dm  S.66: the refusal text comes from !edit_msg, which also knows the shared account's two refusals (11513, 11514).
* 17 Oct 26 dm  S.64, the edit gates: save, save as and new record ask the kernel (edit.check, K$EDIT.OK) before they write into a VOC, a
*               dictionary or a program file, and say why in the message line.
* 14 Oct 26 dm  New (owner: "go ahead and build the ide").  Stages 1 and 2 of the plan in screenlib-design.md
*               together: the shell, open and save, compile with the error table that jumps, run, catalogue,
*               debug, search in the records of a file, a tab for each open record, and a layout that follows
*               the window.
* 19 Oct 26 dm  A click on the menu bar opens the menu under it (SCREEN MENUBAR ... CLICK); it did nothing until Esc had opened the menu.
* 19 Oct 26 dm  The pane that has the focus is named first on the status line (messages 11237-11240), and the editor highlights the character at
*               its cursor while it has the focus (the memo's softcur), for a keyboard user whose terminal shows little else of where the focus is.
* 19 Oct 26 dm  A [*] / [-] indicator in the top right corner of each pane that can have the focus (the owner's request): [*] on the one that has it.
* 23 Oct 26 dm  The AI line (AI line stage 2; $tk_ai): when the AI is usable for the account the form has an AI entry in its last row under the editor (the last stop of the
*               Tab ring; the messages table is one row shorter) and the menu bar an AI menu: Explain the error (the table's row), Explain the selection (the editor's mark
*               to its cursor) and Ask about this record.  Nothing is sent before the preview is answered Send.  An answer may propose edits of the record, shown as a diff in
*               full; Accept changes the EDITOR'S BUFFER as one undo step (Ctrl-Z) and nothing else - saving is the person's own act, through the same save path as ever.
*               Without the AI nothing in the IDE changes.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   IDE {file {record}}
*
*   The account is the project: the tree lists the files of the VOC (type F); Return on a file lists its
*   records (up to 2000) and Return on a record opens it in the editor.  Everything the editor does with a
*   record is the SCREEN MEMO's own; this program adds what a development environment needs around it.
*
*   Save writes the record, and asks first if somebody else changed it since it was read.  Compile saves,
*   runs BASIC and shows the compiler's lines in the table at the bottom; Return on one goes to its line.
*   Run, Catalogue and Debug compile first and do nothing if the compile failed.  Run and Debug give the
*   terminal back to the program and return to the IDE afterwards.  Find searches every record of a file
*   for a text and lists the lines in the same table.
*
*   Nothing here is privileged: the IDE reads and writes records and runs commands exactly as the account's
*   user could from the command prompt, so the per-account gates apply to it as they do there.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $ide
$catalog $ide

$include keyin.h
$include syscom keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include sl.h
$include tk.h

   deffun edit_msg(kind) calling '!edit_msg'      ;* 18 Oct 26 dm, S.66

   prompt ''
   if system(25) then stop                 ;* a phantom has no terminal

   MAXREC = 2000                           ;* records listed for a file
   MAXHIT = 500                            ;* lines a search lists
   LAYW = 140                              ;* what the layout is made for; a bigger window is used whole, a smaller one is refused at
   LAYH = 42                               ;* the start and clipped (upper left corner) if it becomes smaller while the IDE runs

   fn0 = field(@sentence, ' ', 2)
   id0 = field(@sentence, ' ', 3)

   * Ctrl-C and the terminal's break are keys of the editor; both are given back on the way out
   saved.trap = pterm(PT$BREAK, -1)
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, @false)
   break off

   * Line-drawing characters (the frame, the drop-downs, the scroll bars) when the terminal says it is UTF-8 -
   * LC_ALL, then LC_CTYPE, then LANG as a shell resolves them - and + - | otherwise.  Only the borders change.
   loc = env('LC_ALL')
   if loc = '' then loc = env('LC_CTYPE')
   if loc = '' then loc = env('LANG')
   loc = upcase(loc)
   utf8 = (index(loc, 'UTF-8', 1) > 0 or index(loc, 'UTF8', 1) > 0)

   * A window smaller than the layout is refused before the screen is taken (the library's one check and message)
   call $sl_need('IDE', LAYW, LAYH, need.msg, '')
   if need.msg # '' then
      break on
      void pterm(PT$BREAK, saved.trap)
      display need.msg
      stop
   end

   gosub open.screen
   if not(opened) then
      break on
      void pterm(PT$BREAK, saved.trap)
      stop
   end

   * 23 Oct 26: whether the AI is usable for this account (the gate, a provider, a model and a key): without it the IDE has no AI entry, no AI menu and the same layout as ever
   call $tk_ai('usable', '', ai.on, ai.pm)

   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   w = scr<SL.S.W> + 0
   h = scr<SL.S.H> + 0

   * blue first: in classic the bar and the window are the same black on white, so the menu bar and its
   * drop-downs do not stand out from the form under them
   themes = 'blue' : @fm : 'classic' : @fm : 'dark' : @fm : 'light' : @fm : 'mono'
   theme.n = 1
   gosub apply.theme

   * The menus: one message each, entries separated by | and the parts of an entry by ~
   menus = ''
   for j = 11225 to 11230
      if j = 11230 and ai.on then
         m = change(sysmsg(11654), '|', @vm)             ;* the AI menu, before Help
         m = change(m, '~', @sm)
         menus<-1> = m
      end
      m = change(sysmsg(j), '|', @vm)
      m = change(m, '~', @sm)
      menus<-1> = m
   next j

   * State
   nbuf = 0                          ;* the records open: bn (file), bi (record), bu (unique number) in step
   cb = 0                            ;* the one in the editor
   uid = 0
   bn = ''
   bi = ''
   bu = ''
   bx = collection()                 ;* s<uid> the text as it is on disk, t<uid> the text as it was left, c<uid> the cursor
   fl = ''                           ;* the files of the VOC
   lfiles = ''                       ;* the files whose records are listed in the tree
   lrecs = ''                        ;* their record ids, as values, in step
   cur.code = ''
   err.rows = ''                     ;* the table at the bottom
   err.lines = ''
   sid = ''
   sln = ''
   out.kind = ''
   out.file = ''
   focus.id = 'tree'
   msg = ''
   ai.sel = ''                       ;* the AI: the selected text and a sentence about it, for the request being made
   ai.note = ''
   ai.req = ''
   sv.ok = @true
   have.form = @false
   done = @false

   gosub build.files
   if fn0 # '' then
      lf.name = fn0
      gosub load.file
      cur.code = 'F:' : fn0
   end

   gosub new.form
   if fn0 # '' and id0 # '' then
      or.file = fn0
      or.id = id0
      gosub open.record
      screen focus f, 'ed'
      focus.id = 'ed'
   end

   menu.click = ''                          ;* the column of a click on the menu bar, while the menu it opens is being started
   loop
      gosub status.line
      * A second of quiet is an event (timeout), so that the status line follows what is typed: the editor
      * consumes its own keys and nothing else returns to this loop for them
      screen wait f timeout 1 returning ev then
         et = ev<1>
         eid = ev<2>
         edata = ev<3>
         begin case
            case et = 'activate'
               msg = ''
               focus.id = eid
               gosub do.act
            case et = 'focus'
               focus.id = eid
            case et = 'change'
               if eid = 'tabs' then
                  new.cb = edata + 0
                  if new.cb >= 1 and new.cb <= nbuf and new.cb # cb then
                     gosub switch.to
                  end
               end
            case et = 'key'
               gosub do.key
            case et = 'resize'
               gosub relayout
         end case
      end else
         begin case
            case ev<1> = 'end'
               done = @true
            case ev<1> = 'timeout'
               null
            case 1
               cmd = 'menu'             ;* Esc
               gosub run.cmd
         end case
      end
   until done
   repeat

   screen close
   break on
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, saved.trap)
   display @(-1) :
   stop

* ======================================================================
* Themes

apply.theme:
   screen theme themes<theme.n> returning th else
      th = ''
   end
   return

* Out: opened.  The screen is taken, with line-drawing borders if the terminal is UTF-8
open.screen:
   opened = @false
   if utf8 then
      screen open clear lines then
         opened = @true
      end
   end else
      screen open clear then
         opened = @true
      end
   end
   * The cursor lock: when the cursor is off the screen the keys are not the IDE's (it is laid out for LAYW by LAYH)
   if opened then call $sl_guardmode('IDE', LAYW, LAYH)
   return

* ======================================================================
* The files of the account: the VOC entries of type F

build.files:
   fl = ''
   open 'VOC' to vocf then
      sselect vocf to 9
      readlist ids from 9 else ids = ''
      n = dcount(ids, @fm)
      if ids = '' then n = 0
      for i = 1 to n
         read r from vocf, ids<i> then
            * the first field is F, or a description that starts "File" (a file's description is kept there)
            t1 = upcase(r<1>)
            if t1 = 'F' or t1[1, 5] = 'FILE ' then fl<-1> = ids<i>
         end
      next i
   end
   return

* In: lf.name.  The records of the file go into lfiles / lrecs (or are replaced there)
load.file:
   open lf.name to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(11200, lf.name)
      return
   end
   sselect fv to 9
   readlist ids from 9 else ids = ''
   n = dcount(ids, @fm)
   if ids = '' then n = 0
   if n > MAXREC then
      msg = sysmsg(11219, MAXREC, lf.name)
      ids = field(ids, @fm, 1, MAXREC)
   end
   ids = convert(@fm, @vm, ids)
   lp = 0
   nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
   if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
   for q = 1 to nl
      if lfiles<q> = lf.name then
         lp = q
         exit
      end
   next q
   if lp = 0 then
      lfiles<-1> = lf.name
      lrecs<-1> = ids
   end else
      lrecs<lp> = ids
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* The layout from w and h

size.layout:
   lw = w
   lh = h
   if lw < LAYW then lw = LAYW                 ;* the window is smaller only after the start: the layout keeps its size and the
   if lh < LAYH then lh = LAYH                 ;* library cuts what falls outside (the upper left corner shows)
   fw = lw
   fh = lh - 2
   iw = fw - 2
   ih = fh - 2
   tw = 28
   if iw < 90 then tw = int(iw / 3)
   if tw < 14 then tw = 14
   eh = 7
   if ih < 20 then eh = 4
   ex = tw + 1
   ew = iw - tw
   mh = ih - 1 - eh
   err.y = 2 + mh
   ai.rows = (if ai.on then 1 else 0)          ;* the AI entry takes the last row of the form under the editor and the table: the table is one row shorter
   ai.y = ih
   return

* The form and everything on it, from the state
new.form:
   last.status = char(1)                ;* the screen is made again: the status line has to be written (status.line writes only a changed text)
   lc.nodraw = @false                   ;* see load.current
   did.focus = @false
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   w = scr<SL.S.W> + 0
   h = scr<SL.S.H> + 0
   * 19 Oct 26 dm - the layout is made for the WINDOW as well as the display, like SED, CED and the debugger (a display of 200 by 60 typed with TERM on a
   *   140 by 42 window made the IDE lay out 200 by 60, which scrolled and garbled the screen): the smaller of the two.
   call $sl_window(ww0, wh0)
   if ww0 < w then w = ww0
   if wh0 < h then h = wh0
   gosub size.layout
   t = sysmsg(11199, @who)
   screen form title t at 0,1 size fw,fh returning f
   have.form = @true

   gosub tree.nodes
   screen tree f, 'tree', nodes at 1,1 size tw - 1,ih value cur.k

   * A vertical line between the tree and the editor, from the top of the form to its bottom, in the colours of the
   * form's own frame (a line-drawing character on a UTF-8 terminal, | otherwise)
   call $sl_line('v', '', vch)
   for r = 1 to ih
      screen label f, 'sep' : r, vch at tw,r width 1 attr 'window'
   next r

   * The strip of tabs on a bar of its own, as wide as the editor
   screen label f, 'tabbar', ' ' at ex,1 width ew attr 'tab'
   gosub tab.labels
   screen tabs f, 'tabs', labels at ex,1 value tab.n

   ed.text = ''
   ed.syn = ''
   ed.ro = 1
   if nbuf > 0 then
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 't' : u
      ed.text = bx{k}
      ed.ro = 0
      sy.file = bn<cb>
      sy.id = bi<cb>
      gosub syntax.for
      ed.syn = syn
   end
   if ed.ro then
      screen memo f, 'ed', ed.text at ex,2 size ew,mh syntax ed.syn lnum readonly
   end else
      screen memo f, 'ed', ed.text at ex,2 size ew,mh syntax ed.syn lnum
   end
   * 18 Oct 26 dm - a horizontal scroll bar in the editor's last row (the owner: "there is no horizontal scroll bar"); the text has one row less
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   mw{'hbar'} = 1
   mw{'passkeys'} = 'ctrl-e'                     ;* Ctrl-E hands the record to SED (the area's own meaning of it, end of row, is End)
   mw{'softcur'} = 1                             ;* 19 Oct 26: the character under the cursor is highlighted while the editor has the focus

   cols = sysmsg(11221) : @sm : '6R' : @vm : sysmsg(11222) : @sm : (ew - 11) : 'L'
   * A bar of its own above the table: the table is drawn like the editor (the text style), and with nothing
   * between them the messages read as the end of the editor
   screen label f, 'errbar', sysmsg(11231) at ex,err.y width ew attr 'window'
   screen table f, 'err', cols, err.rows at ex,err.y + 1 size ew,eh - 1 - ai.rows
   if ai.on then
      * 23 Oct 26: the AI line, the last stop of the Tab ring: a label and an entry in the form's last row, Return in it is an event of its own (aw{'activate'})
      screen label f, 'ailab', sysmsg(11636) at ex,ai.y width 4 attr 'window'
      screen entry f, 'ai' at ex + 4,ai.y width ew - 4 maxlen 300
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ai', an)
      aw = f{'widgets', an}
      aw{'activate'} = 1
   end
   * Return in the tree and in the table is an event of its own (activate), not the press of a default button
   f{'activate'} = 1
   f{'bgclick'} = 1                     ;* a left click on no widget (the menu bar) comes back as the key mouse: the bar is the IDE's, not a widget

   * 19 Oct 26 dm - the owner: "a small visual indicator in the top right corner of each pane to indicate that it is active, [-] for off, [*] for on".
   *   One three-cell label to a pane that can have the focus, added last so that it is drawn over the pane's own top edge: the files on the form's top
   *   border above the tree, the editor on the border above it, the tabs at the right end of their strip, the messages at the right end of their bar.
   ind.ids = 'ixtree' : @fm : 'ixtabs' : @fm : 'ixed' : @fm : 'ixerr'
   ind.panes = 'tree' : @fm : 'tabs' : @fm : 'ed' : @fm : 'err'
   gosub indicator.texts
   screen label f, 'ixtree', ind.want<1> at tw - 4,0 width 3 attr 'window'
   screen label f, 'ixtabs', ind.want<2> at ex + ew - 3,1 width 3 attr 'tab'
   screen label f, 'ixed', ind.want<3> at ex + ew - 4,0 width 3 attr 'window'
   screen label f, 'ixerr', ind.want<4> at ex + ew - 3,err.y width 3 attr 'window'
   ind.cur = ind.want

   gosub tree.apply
   if nbuf > 0 then gosub memo.cursor
   screen focus f, focus.id
   screen menubar menus row 0 draw else null
   return

* The nodes of the tree (a file, and under it the records of a file that is loaded), the numbers of the open ones
* and the number of the node whose code is cur.code
tree.nodes:
   nodes = ''
   open.list = ''
   cur.k = 1
   nn = 0
   nf = dcount(fl, @fm)
   if fl = '' then nf = 0
   for i = 1 to nf
      nm = fl<i>
      nn += 1
      nodes<-1> = nm : @sm : 1 : @sm : 'F:' : nm
      if 'F:' : nm = cur.code then cur.k = nn
      lp = 0
      nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
      if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
      for q = 1 to nl
         if lfiles<q> = nm then
            lp = q
            exit
         end
      next q
      if lp then
         open.list<1, -1> = nn
         ids = lrecs<lp>
         ni = dcount(ids, @vm)
         if ids = '' then ni = 0
         for j = 1 to ni
            nn += 1
            c2 = 'R:' : nm : ':' : ids<1, j>
            nodes<-1> = ids<1, j> : @sm : 2 : @sm : c2
            if c2 = cur.code then cur.k = nn
         next j
      end
   next i
   return

* The tree widget takes the nodes again, keeping the current one
tree.apply:
   gosub tree.nodes
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'tree', tn)
   if tn = 0 then return
   tr = f{'widgets', tn}
   tr{'items'} = convert(@fm, @vm, nodes)
   tr{'cur'} = cur.k
   tr{'open'} = open.list
   tr{'opened'} = 1
   call $sl_widget_init(tr)
   return

tab.labels:
   labels = ''
   for i = 1 to nbuf
      labels<1, i> = bi<i>
   next i
   if nbuf = 0 then labels = sysmsg(11220)
   tab.n = cb
   if tab.n < 1 then tab.n = 1
   return

* In: sy.file, sy.id.  Out: syn, the language the editor colours the record as ('' for none)
syntax.for:
   syn = ''
   r = downcase(sy.id)
   n = dcount(r, '.')
   suffix = ''
   if n > 1 then suffix = field(r, '.', n)
   begin case
      case suffix = 'json'
         syn = 'json'
      case suffix = 'c' or suffix = 'h'
         syn = 'c'
      case suffix = 'py'
         syn = 'python'
      case suffix = 'md'
         syn = 'markdown'
      case suffix = 'sh'
         syn = 'sh'
      case index(downcase(sy.file), 'bp', 1)
         syn = 'sd.basic'
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* The editor and the buffers

* Keep what the editor holds (text and cursor) with the record that is open
stash.current:
   if nbuf > 0 and have.form then
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      mw = f{'widgets', en}
      screen get f, 'ed' returning txt
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 't' : u
      bx{k} = txt
      k = 'c' : u
      bx{k} = mw{'cx'} : @vm : mw{'cy'} : @vm : mw{'top'} : @vm : mw{'left'}
   end
   return

* The editor shows the record cb: its text, its language, its cursor; the tab strip says which
load.current:
   gosub tab.labels
   f{'quiet'} = 1                        ;* SCREEN SET draws the whole form after each set: the strip and the editor are drawn below, alone
   screen set f, 'tabs' items labels
   screen set f, 'tabs' value tab.n
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   if nbuf > 0 then
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 't' : u
      mw{'value'} = bx{k}
      sy.file = bn<cb>
      sy.id = bi<cb>
      gosub syntax.for
      ro = 0
   end else
      mw{'value'} = ''
      syn = ''
      ro = 1
   end
   call $sl_widget_init(mw)
   nl = 0
   if syn # '' then call $sl_syntax('index', syn, '', '', '', nl)
   mw{'synlang'} = nl + 0
   mw{'readonly'} = ro
   if nbuf > 0 then gosub memo.cursor
   * 18 Oct 26 dm - only the tab strip and the editor have changed: drawing the whole form again wrote every cell of the screen, which the
   *   owner saw as a flash each time a record was chosen ("flashes the whole screen").  A caller that is about to focus the editor (which
   *   draws it) sets lc.nodraw and draws the tab strip itself.
   if not(lc.nodraw) then gosub redraw.editor
   return

* The cursor of the record cb goes back where it was
memo.cursor:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   u = bu<cb>
   k = 'c' : u
   cc = bx{k}
   if cc # '' then
      k = 't' : u
      nl = dcount(bx{k}, @fm)
      if cc<1, 2> + 0 >= 1 and cc<1, 2> + 0 <= nl then
         mw{'cx'} = cc<1, 1> + 0
         mw{'cy'} = cc<1, 2> + 0
         mw{'top'} = cc<1, 3> + 0
         mw{'left'} = cc<1, 4> + 0
         mw{'want'} = cc<1, 1> + 0
      end
   end
   return

redraw:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('redraw', f, scr, '', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* The tab strip and the editor only (a record chosen, a tab closed or switched); the rest of the screen is as it was
redraw.editor:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabbar', rr)       ;* the blank bar under the strip: a strip that got shorter leaves no label behind
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabs', rr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'ed', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* A file name and a record id for a command line: qp.file and qp.id in, qp.f and qp.i out, each in quotes when it holds a blank (the command
* parser reads one word to a name otherwise, and the verifier's Ctrl-E on the record "my rec" opened a new record "my" in SED).  A name that
* holds both kinds of quote is left as it is.
quote.pair:
   qp.f = qp.file
   qp.i = qp.id
   for qp.k = 1 to 2
      qp.v = if qp.k = 1 then qp.file else qp.id
      if index(qp.v, ' ', 1) or index(qp.v, '"', 1) or index(qp.v, "'", 1) then
         if not(index(qp.v, '"', 1)) then
            qp.v = '"' : qp.v : '"'
         end else
            if not(index(qp.v, "'", 1)) then qp.v = "'" : qp.v : "'"
         end
      end
      if qp.k = 1 then qp.f = qp.v else qp.i = qp.v
   next qp.k
   return

* The focus goes to widget ft.id and is shown there: the widget that had it and the one that has it are drawn, and the cursor goes to the new one
focus.to:
   focus.id = ft.id
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('focus', f, scr, ft.id, rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

redraw.tabs:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabbar', rr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabs', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* The tree only (a file opened or closed in it)
redraw.tree:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tree', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* In: or.file, or.id.  Opens the record in a tab of its own, or goes to the tab it is already in
open.record:
   p = 0
   for i = 1 to nbuf
      if bn<i> = or.file and bi<i> = or.id then
         p = i
         exit
      end
   next i
   if p then
      new.cb = p
      if new.cb # cb then gosub switch.to
      return
   end
   open or.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(11200, or.file)
      return
   end
   read rec from fv, or.id else
      msg = sysmsg(11201, or.id, or.file)
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   nbuf += 1
   uid += 1
   bn<nbuf> = or.file
   bi<nbuf> = or.id
   bu<nbuf> = uid
   k = 's' : uid
   bx{k} = rec
   k = 't' : uid
   bx{k} = rec
   k = 'c' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   cb = nbuf
   gosub load.current
   return

* In: new.cb
switch.to:
   gosub stash.current
   cb = new.cb
   gosub load.current
   return

* Whether record i has changes: dirty
is.dirty:
   u = bu<i>
   k = 't' : u
   d1 = bx{k}
   k = 's' : u
   d2 = bx{k}
   dirty = (d1 # d2)
   return

* ======================================================================
* The pane indicators: [*] on the pane that has the focus (focus.id), [-] on the others

* ind.want = the four texts, in the order of ind.panes
indicator.texts:
   ind.want = ''
   for ix = 1 to 4
      ind.want<ix> = if focus.id = ind.panes<ix> then '[*]' else '[-]'
   next ix
   return

* The ones that changed are set and drawn again, alone: the form is not drawn again (f{'quiet'}), and a label that has not changed is not touched
set.indicators:
   gosub indicator.texts
   if ind.want = ind.cur then return
   f{'quiet'} = 1
   for ix = 1 to 4
      if ind.want<ix> # ind.cur<ix> then screen set f, ind.ids<ix> text ind.want<ix>
   next ix
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   for ix = 1 to 4
      if ind.want<ix> # ind.cur<ix> then call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, ind.ids<ix>, rr)
   next ix
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   ind.cur = ind.want
   return

* ======================================================================
* The status line, and the bar

status.line:
   gosub set.indicators
   t = ''
   if nbuf > 0 then
      gosub stash.current
      i = cb
      gosub is.dirty
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      mw = f{'widgets', en}
      t = bn<cb> : ' ' : bi<cb>
      if dirty then t := ' *'
      t := '   ' : sysmsg(11213, mw{'cy'}, mw{'cx'})
   end
   if msg # '' then t := '   ' : msg
   * 18 Oct 26 dm - SCREEN TEXT leaves the cursor after the text, so the cursor stayed at the end of the status row and the editor showed none
   *   (found by the owner: "no cursor when it is first started"; the loop writes this line once a second and on every key).  It is put back
   *   where the widget with the focus had left it.
   * The line is written only when its text has changed since it was last written: the loop comes here about ten times a second, and an idle IDE
   *   wrote 140 cells each time (new.form, which makes the screen again, forgets the text so that the line is written then)
   * 19 Oct 26 dm - which pane has the focus, first on the line (the owner, using the keyboard only: "no visible indication of which box is active"):
   *   Tab moves the focus from the files to the tabs, the editor and the messages, and the terminal's own cursor is the only other sign of it
   begin case
      case focus.id = 'tree'
         pn = sysmsg(11237)
      case focus.id = 'tabs'
         pn = sysmsg(11238)
      case focus.id = 'ed'
         pn = sysmsg(11239)
      case focus.id = 'err'
         pn = sysmsg(11240)
      case focus.id = 'ai'
         pn = 'AI'
      case 1
         pn = ''
   end case
   if pn # '' then t = '[' : pn : ']' : (if t = '' then '' else '  ' : t)
   t = fmt(t[1, lw], lw : 'L')
   if t = last.status then return
   last.status = t
   keep.cur = ''
   call $sl_curat('get', keep.cur)
   screen text t at 0,lh - 1 attr 'reverse'     ;* the layout's last row: off the screen in a smaller window
   call $sl_curat('back', keep.cur)
   return

* ======================================================================
* Keys, the menu and the commands

do.key:
   msg = ''
   cmd = ''
   begin case
      case edata = 'f1'
         cmd = 'help'
      case edata = 'f2' or edata = 'ctrl-s'
         cmd = 'save'
      case edata = 'f3'
         cmd = 'tree'
      case edata = 'f4'
         cmd = 'nexterr'
      case edata = 'f5'
         cmd = 'run'
      case edata = 'f6'
         cmd = 'nexttab'
      case edata = 'f7'
         cmd = 'compile'
      case edata = 'f8'
         cmd = 'catalog'
      case edata = 'f9'
         cmd = 'debug'
      case edata = 'f10'
         cmd = 'menu'
      case edata = 'ctrl-q'
         cmd = 'quit'
      case edata = 'ctrl-w'
         cmd = 'writeas'                 ;* SED's write record
      case edata = 'ctrl-e'
         cmd = 'sed'                     ;* the record goes to SED and comes back (the editor leaves this key to the IDE: passkeys)
      case edata = 'ctrl-l'
         cmd = 'goto'
      case edata = 'ctrl-r'
         cmd = 'replace'                 ;* SED's replace
      case edata = 'f11'
         cmd = 'find'                    ;* in the records of the file
      case edata = 'guardquit'
         done = @true                    ;* the user left from the cursor lock ($sl_guardmode): nothing is saved
      case edata = 'mouse'
         * The bar is not a widget, so a click on it comes here as a key the form had no use for.  19 Oct 26 dm: the left button on the bar's
         * row (0) opens the menu whose title is under it; the menu was opened only by F10 or Esc before, and a click did nothing until then.
         call $sl_mouse('get', mev)
         if mev<1> = 'press' and mev<2> = 1 and mev<4> = 0 then
            menu.click = mev<3>
            cmd = 'menu'
         end
   end case
   if cmd # '' then gosub run.cmd
   return

run.cmd:
   begin case
      case cmd = 'menu'
         mk = menu.click
         menu.click = ''
         if mk = '' then
            screen menubar menus returning mc then
               cmd = mc
               gosub run.cmd
            end
         end else
            screen menubar menus click mk returning mc then      ;* the menu under the click; the space beside the titles opens nothing
               cmd = mc
               gosub run.cmd
            end
         end
      case cmd = 'help'
         s = convert(char(13), '', sysmsg(11214) : sysmsg(11215) : sysmsg(11216))
         s = change(s, char(10), @vm)
         screen msgbox s returning b else b = 0
      case cmd = 'save'
         gosub save.current
      case cmd = 'tree'
         ft.id = 'tree'                    ;* SCREEN FOCUS moves the pointer and draws nothing: the form's own op shows the change
         gosub focus.to
      case cmd = 'nexterr'
         gosub next.error
      case cmd = 'nexttab'
         if nbuf > 1 then
            new.cb = cb + 1
            if new.cb > nbuf then new.cb = 1
            gosub switch.to
         end
      case cmd = 'compile'
         c.opt = ''
         gosub do.compile
      case cmd = 'catalog'
         c.opt = ''
         gosub do.compile
         if c.ok then
            qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
            execute 'catalog ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i capturing co
            msg = sysmsg(11207, c.file, c.id)
         end
      case cmd = 'run'
         c.opt = ''
         gosub do.compile
         if c.ok then
            gosub leave.screen
            qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
            execute 'run ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i trapping aborts
            gosub retake.screen
         end
      case cmd = 'debug'
         c.opt = ' debugging'
         gosub do.compile
         if c.ok then
            gosub leave.screen
            qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
            execute 'debug ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i trapping aborts
            gosub retake.screen
         end
      case cmd = 'close'
         gosub close.current
      case cmd = 'sed'
         gosub do.sed
      case cmd = 'quit'
         gosub do.quit
      case cmd = 'goto'
         if nbuf = 0 then
            msg = sysmsg(11218)
         end else
            screen ask sysmsg(11209) returning gl.text then
               if num(gl.text) and gl.text # '' then
                  gl.n = gl.text + 0
                  gosub goto.line
               end
            end
         end
      case cmd = 'find'
         gosub do.find
      case cmd = 'writeas'
         gosub do.writeas
      case cmd = 'replace'
         gosub do.replace
      case cmd = 'new'
         gosub do.new
      case cmd = 'ai.error'
         gosub ai.error
      case cmd = 'ai.sel'
         gosub ai.selection
      case cmd = 'ai.ask'
         if nbuf = 0 then
            msg = sysmsg(11662)
         end else
            ft.id = 'ai'                  ;* the entry has the focus: the person types the request there
            gosub focus.to
            msg = sysmsg(11655)
         end
      case cmd = 'theme'
         theme.n += 1
         if theme.n > dcount(themes, @fm) then theme.n = 1
         gosub apply.theme
         screen refresh
         last.status = char(1)          ;* everything was drawn again, the status line too
   end case
   return

* Return in the tree, the table or the tab strip (the form's default button)
do.act:
   did.focus = @false
   begin case
      case focus.id = 'tree'
         screen get f, 'tree' returning code
         begin case
            case code[1, 2] = 'F:'
               lf.name = code[3, 99]
               lp = 0
               nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
               if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
               for q = 1 to nl
                  if lfiles<q> = lf.name then
                     lp = q
                     exit
                  end
               next q
               if lp then
                  del lfiles<lp>
                  del lrecs<lp>
               end else
                  gosub load.file
               end
               cur.code = code
               gosub tree.apply
               gosub redraw.tree
            case code[1, 2] = 'R:'
               or.file = field(code, ':', 2)
               or.id = code[len(or.file) + 4, 9999]
               cur.code = code
               * 18 Oct 26 dm - the editor is drawn once: by the focus that follows, not also by open.record (which drew the whole form, and then
               *   again at the end of this routine).  SCREEN FOCUS only moves the form's pointer and draws nothing, so the focus goes through the
               *   form's own op, which draws the widget that had it (the tree) and the one that has it (the editor, with the cursor in its text).
               lc.nodraw = @true
               gosub open.record
               lc.nodraw = @false
               if nbuf > 0 then
                  ft.id = 'ed'
                  gosub focus.to
                  did.focus = @true
                  gosub redraw.tabs
               end
         end case
      case focus.id = 'err'
         screen get f, 'err' returning rn
         if rn # '' then
            k2 = rn + 0
            gosub act.error
         end
      case focus.id = 'ai'
         * Return in the AI entry: the request is what was typed; the whole record is the text, the page says where the cursor is
         ai.req = edata
         ai.kind = 'ask'
         gosub ai.go
      case 1
         null
   end case
   if not(did.focus) then screen focus f, focus.id
   return

* The row k2 of the table: go to what it names
act.error:
   if k2 < 1 or k2 > dcount(err.rows, @fm) then return
   if out.kind = 'search' then
      or.file = out.file
      or.id = sid<k2>
      gosub open.record
      gl.n = sln<k2> + 0
   end else
      gl.n = err.lines<k2> + 0
   end
   if gl.n >= 1 and nbuf > 0 then
      gosub goto.line
   end
   return

* In: gl.n.  The cursor of the editor goes to that line, which is brought to the middle of the window
goto.line:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   nl = dcount(mw{'value'}, @fm)
   if gl.n > nl then gl.n = nl
   if gl.n < 1 then gl.n = 1
   mw{'cy'} = gl.n
   mw{'cx'} = 1
   mw{'want'} = 1
   mw{'left'} = 1
   mw{'mx'} = 0
   mw{'my'} = 0
   tp = gl.n - int(mh / 2)
   if tp < 1 then tp = 1
   mw{'top'} = tp
   ft.id = 'ed'                         ;* the widget that had the focus and the editor are drawn, the cursor in the editor
   gosub focus.to
   return

* F4: the row after the current one of the table, and what it names
next.error:
   n = dcount(err.rows, @fm)
   if err.rows = '' then n = 0
   if n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11218)
      return
   end
   screen get f, 'err' returning rn
   k2 = rn + 1
   if k2 > n then k2 = 1
   gosub select.row
   gosub act.error
   return

* The table's current row is k2, shown near the middle
select.row:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'err', tn)
   tt = f{'widgets', tn}
   tt{'cur'} = k2
   tt{'value'} = k2
   tp = k2 - int((eh - 2) / 2)
   if tp < 1 then tp = 1
   tt{'top'} = tp
   gosub redraw
   return

* The table shows err.rows
err.load:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'err', tn)
   tt = f{'widgets', tn}
   tt{'rows'} = err.rows
   tt{'top'} = 1
   tt{'cur'} = 1
   tt{'value'} = 1
   tt{'picked'} = ''
   call $sl_widget_init(tt)
   gosub redraw
   return

* ======================================================================
* Saving, closing, quitting

* Save the record in the editor
save.current:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11218)
      sv.ok = @false
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   sv.i = cb
   gosub save.one
   return

* edit.check  -  may this session save a change to the file in fv?  (17 Oct 26 dm, S.64)
*   IDE is $internal, so the kernel's own test of a WRITE to a VOC, a dictionary or a program file lets it through; it asks for itself,
*   by the pathname of the OPEN file (K$EDIT.OK).  The reason goes in msg, as every IDE complaint does (DISPLAY would write over the
*   screen).  Out: edit.ok.
edit.check:
   edit.ok = @true
   edit.p = fileinfo(fv, FL$PATH)
   if kernel(K$EDIT.OK, edit.p) then return
   edit.ok = @false
   edit.kind = kernel(K$EDIT.KIND, edit.p)
   msg = edit_msg(edit.kind) ;* 11405 VOC, 11406 dictionary, 11407 program file, 11513 shared record, 11514 shared structure
   return

* In: sv.i.  Out: sv.ok
save.one:
   sv.ok = @false
   u = bu<sv.i>
   k = 't' : u
   sv.text = bx{k}
   k = 's' : u
   sv.saved = bx{k}
   if sv.text = sv.saved then
      sv.ok = @true
      return
   end
   open bn<sv.i> to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(11200, bn<sv.i>)
      return
   end
   gosub edit.check                  ;* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64
   if not(edit.ok) then return
   read disk from fv, bi<sv.i> then
      if disk # sv.saved then
         screen msgbox sysmsg(11203, bn<sv.i>, bi<sv.i>) buttons 'Overwrite,Cancel' default 2 returning b else b = 0
         if b # 1 then return
      end
   end else
      null
   end
   write sv.text on fv, bi<sv.i>
   k = 's' : u
   bx{k} = sv.text
   sv.ok = @true
   msg = sysmsg(11202, bn<sv.i>, bi<sv.i>)
   return

close.current:
   if nbuf = 0 then return
   gosub stash.current
   i = cb
   gosub is.dirty
   if dirty then
      screen msgbox sysmsg(11204, bn<cb>, bi<cb>) buttons 'Save,Discard,Cancel' default 1 returning b else b = 3
      begin case
         case b = 1
            sv.i = cb
            gosub save.one
            if not(sv.ok) then return
         case b = 2
            null
         case 1
            return
      end case
   end
   del bn<cb>
   del bi<cb>
   del bu<cb>
   nbuf -= 1
   if cb > nbuf then cb = nbuf
   if cb < 1 then cb = 1
   gosub load.current
   return

do.quit:
   gosub stash.current
   abort = @false
   for i = 1 to nbuf
      gosub is.dirty
      if dirty then
         screen msgbox sysmsg(11204, bn<i>, bi<i>) buttons 'Save,Discard,Cancel' default 1 returning b else b = 3
         begin case
            case b = 1
               sv.i = i
               gosub save.one
               if not(sv.ok) then abort = @true
            case b = 2
               null
            case 1
               abort = @true
         end case
      end
   until abort
   next i
   if not(abort) then done = @true
   return

* ======================================================================
* Building

* In: c.opt ('' or ' debugging').  Out: c.ok, c.file, c.id; the table holds the compiler's lines
do.compile:
   c.ok = @false
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11218)
      return
   end
   gosub save.current
   if not(sv.ok) then return
   c.file = bn<cb>
   c.id = bi<cb>
   qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
   execute 'basic ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i : c.opt capturing c.out
   c.ok = (@system.return.code # 0)
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   nerr = 0
   loop
      z = remove(c.out, delim)
      begin case
         case z matches "1N...:..."
            ln = matchfield(z, '0N0X', 1) + 0
            tx = trimf(field(z, ':', 2, 999))
            nerr += 1
            err.rows<-1> = ln : @vm : tx
            err.lines<-1> = ln
         case z matches "'WARNING:'0X"
            nerr += 1
            err.rows<-1> = 0 : @vm : trimf(z)
            err.lines<-1> = 0
      end case
   while delim
   repeat
   out.kind = 'errors'
   out.file = c.file
   gosub err.load
   if c.ok then
      msg = sysmsg(11205, c.file, c.id)
   end else
      msg = sysmsg(11206, nerr, c.file, c.id)
      n = dcount(err.lines, @fm)
      for q = 1 to n
         if err.lines<q> + 0 > 0 then
            k2 = q
            gosub select.row
            gosub act.error
            exit
         end
      next q
   end
   return

* The terminal goes back to the program being run, and comes back to the IDE after it
leave.screen:
   screen close
   break on
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, saved.trap)
   display @(-1) :
   return

retake.screen:
   display sysmsg(11223)
   input junk
* Without the pause (SED handed the record back and has nothing left to be read): the same, straight on
retake.quiet:
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, @false)
   break off
   gosub open.screen
   gosub apply.theme
   gosub new.form
   return

* ======================================================================
* Ctrl-E, File / Edit in SED: the record goes to SED and comes back (Part B of the 140x42 plan).  SED edits the record ON DISK, so a changed
* buffer is saved first (asked; Cancel leaves everything as it is).  SED ends its edit at its first successful save (it is told so by the
* named common /sedide/, see gpl.bp/sed), or the user leaves it without saving; the IDE then reads the record again (the disk is the truth),
* forgets the compile errors (they named lines of the old text), takes the screen back WITHOUT the "press Return" pause of Run, and says
* which it was.  SED makes ide.call 2 once the record is open for editing: if it is still 1 on the way back SED stopped on a message of its
* own (a record locked by another session, a refusal), and the IDE waits for a key so that the message can be read.
do.sed:
   common /sedide/ ide.call
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11218)
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   i = cb
   gosub is.dirty
   if dirty then
      screen msgbox sysmsg(11603, bn<cb>, bi<cb>) buttons 'Save,Cancel' default 1 returning b else b = 2
      if b # 1 then return
      sv.i = cb
      gosub save.one
      if not(sv.ok) then return
   end
   sd.file = bn<cb>
   sd.id = bi<cb>
   sd.u = bu<cb>
   k = 's' : sd.u
   sd.was = bx{k}
   gosub leave.screen
   ide.call = 1
   qp.file = sd.file ; qp.id = sd.id ; gosub quote.pair
   execute 'sed ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i trapping aborts
   sd.used = (ide.call = 2)
   ide.call = 0
   disk = sd.was
   open sd.file to fv then
      read disk from fv, sd.id else disk = sd.was
   end
   k = 's' : sd.u
   bx{k} = disk
   k = 't' : sd.u
   bx{k} = disk
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   out.kind = ''
   if sd.used then gosub retake.quiet else gosub retake.screen
   if disk # sd.was then msg = sysmsg(11604, sd.file, sd.id) else msg = sysmsg(11605, sd.file, sd.id)
   return

* The window changed size: the form is made again at the new size from the state
relayout:
   gosub stash.current
   screen clear
   gosub new.form
   return

* ======================================================================
* Records: a new one, and a search

* ======================================================================
* The AI (23 Oct 26, AI line stage 2).  $tk_ai does the asking: the preview of everything that will be sent (Send or Cancel, Cancel the default), the wait, the answer and, if the
* answer proposes edits, the diff in full with Accept and Reject (Reject the default).  This program supplies what it may be told and applies what is accepted to ITS OWN BUFFER, as
* one undo step; it saves nothing (Save is the person's act, through save.current and the kernel's edit gates as ever).

* Explain the compile message the table has selected (the table must hold a compile, not a search)
ai.error:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11662)
      return
   end
   if err.rows = '' or out.kind # 'errors' then
      msg = sysmsg(11663)
      return
   end
   screen get f, 'err' returning ai.rn
   ai.k = ai.rn + 0
   if ai.k < 1 or ai.k > dcount(err.rows, @fm) then
      msg = sysmsg(11663)
      return
   end
   ai.req = sysmsg(11656, err.rows<ai.k, 2>)
   ai.kind = 'error'
   ai.note = 'The compile message: ' : err.rows<ai.k, 2> : (if err.lines<ai.k> + 0 > 0 then ' (line ' : err.lines<ai.k> : ')' else '')
   gosub ai.go
   return

* Explain the text from the editor's mark to its cursor
ai.selection:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11662)
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ai.my = mw{'my'} + 0
   ai.mx = mw{'mx'} + 0
   if ai.my < 1 or ai.mx < 1 then
      msg = sysmsg(11664)
      return
   end
   ai.cy = mw{'cy'} + 0
   ai.cx = mw{'cx'} + 0
   * the earlier end first
   if ai.my < ai.cy or (ai.my = ai.cy and ai.mx <= ai.cx) then
      ai.y1 = ai.my ; ai.x1 = ai.mx ; ai.y2 = ai.cy ; ai.x2 = ai.cx
   end else
      ai.y1 = ai.cy ; ai.x1 = ai.cx ; ai.y2 = ai.my ; ai.x2 = ai.mx
   end
   ai.lines = mw{'value'}
   ai.sel = ''
   for ai.q = ai.y1 to ai.y2
      ai.ln = ai.lines<ai.q>
      begin case
         case ai.y1 = ai.y2
            ai.part = ai.ln[ai.x1, ai.x2 - ai.x1]
         case ai.q = ai.y1
            ai.part = ai.ln[ai.x1, 99999]
         case ai.q = ai.y2
            ai.part = ai.ln[1, ai.x2 - 1]
         case 1
            ai.part = ai.ln
      end case
      ai.sel := (if ai.q = ai.y1 then '' else char(10)) : ai.part
   next ai.q
   if ai.sel = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(11664)
      return
   end
   ai.req = sysmsg(11657)
   ai.kind = 'selection'
   ai.note = 'The selection runs from line ' : ai.y1 : ' to line ' : ai.y2
   gosub ai.go
   return

* In: ai.req (the request), ai.kind, ai.note (a sentence about what is selected, or null), ai.sel (the selected text, or null).  The record in the editor, as far as it fits, is the text
ai.go:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11662)
      return
   end
   if trim(ai.req) = '' then return
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ai.lines = mw{'value'}
   ai.cy = mw{'cy'} + 0
   ai.total = dcount(ai.lines, @fm)
   * the window: the whole record when it fits in 24000 bytes, else lines round the cursor
   ai.first = 1
   ai.last = ai.total
   if len(ai.lines) > 24000 then
      ai.first = ai.cy - 150
      if ai.first < 1 then ai.first = 1
      ai.last = ai.first
      ai.size = 0
      loop
      while ai.last <= ai.total and ai.size + len(ai.lines<ai.last>) + 1 <= 24000
         ai.size += len(ai.lines<ai.last>) + 1
         ai.last += 1
      repeat
      ai.last -= 1
      if ai.last < ai.first then ai.last = ai.first
   end
   ai.off = 0                                              ;* the number of characters before the window in the whole text (a line feed after each line)
   for ai.q = 1 to ai.first - 1
      ai.off += len(ai.lines<ai.q>) + 1
   next ai.q
   ai.win = ''
   for ai.q = ai.first to ai.last
      ai.win := (if ai.q = ai.first then '' else char(10)) : ai.lines<ai.q>
   next ai.q
   ai.win = convert(@vm : @sm : @tm, '???', ai.win)       ;* a mark in a line is shown to the model (and offered to nobody) as ?, one for one
   ai.win = ai.win[1, 24000]
   page = 'This is the IDE of SD Core, an editor for SD BASIC programs and other records.'
   page<1, -1> = 'File ' : bn<cb> : ', record ' : bi<cb> : '.'
   page<1, -1> = 'The cursor is on line ' : ai.cy : ' of ' : ai.total : '.'
   if ai.note # '' then page<1, -1> = ai.note
   if ai.first > 1 or ai.last < ai.total then msg = sysmsg(11667, ai.first, ai.last - ai.first + 1)
   actx = 'IDE' : @fm : 'ide' : @fm : page : @fm : '' : @fm : 0 : @fm : ai.req : @fm : lh - 1 : @fm : ai.win : @fm : ai.first : @fm : ai.sel : @fm : 'nocmd'
   ai.ctx = ''
   ai.ctx<TK.C.W> = lw
   res = ''
   call $tk_ai('ask', ai.ctx, actx, res)
   last.status = char(1)                                   ;* the wait wrote on the status row, and the preview over the form: both are drawn again
   if res<5> # '' then msg = res<5>
   if res<1> = 'edit' then gosub ai.apply
   if focus.id = 'ai' then
      screen set f, 'ai' value ''
   end
   ai.sel = ''
   ai.note = ''
   gosub redraw
   return

* The accepted edits (res<2>: position SM length SM new text, as values, positions in the window) go into the editor's buffer, as ONE undo step.  The buffer must still hold, at each
* place, exactly what the model was shown; if the person changed it while the AI was answering, nothing is applied.  Nothing is written to the disk.
ai.apply:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ai.now = change(mw{'value'}, @fm, char(10))
   ai.n = dcount(res<2>, @vm)
   ai.fits = (ai.n > 0)
   for ai.k = 1 to ai.n
      ai.p = res<2, ai.k, 1> + 0
      ai.l = res<2, ai.k, 2> + 0
      if ai.now[ai.off + ai.p, ai.l] # ai.win[ai.p, ai.l] then ai.fits = 0
   next ai.k
   if not(ai.fits) then
      msg = sysmsg(11666)
      return
   end
   ai.new = ai.now
   for ai.k = ai.n to 1 step -1
      ai.p = res<2, ai.k, 1> + 0
      ai.l = res<2, ai.k, 2> + 0
      ai.new = ai.new[1, ai.off + ai.p - 1] : res<2, ai.k, 3> : ai.new[ai.off + ai.p + ai.l, 99999999]
   next ai.k
   ai.p1 = res<2, 1, 1> + 0
   ai.line1 = 1
   if ai.off + ai.p1 > 1 then ai.line1 = count(ai.now[1, ai.off + ai.p1 - 1], char(10)) + 1
   * the memo's own undo stack: the old text and cursor go on the front, as its snap.make has them, and redo is forgotten
   ai.snap = mw{'cy'} : @tm : mw{'cx'} : @tm : convert(@fm, @vm, mw{'value'})
   mw{'undo'} = ai.snap : @fm : mw{'undo'}
   mw{'redo'} = ''
   mw{'value'} = change(ai.new, char(10), @fm)
   mw{'lastop'} = ''
   mw{'cy'} = ai.line1
   mw{'cx'} = 1
   mw{'want'} = 1
   mw{'left'} = 1
   mw{'mx'} = 0
   mw{'my'} = 0
   ai.tp = ai.line1 - int(mh / 2)
   if ai.tp < 1 then ai.tp = 1
   mw{'top'} = ai.tp
   msg = sysmsg(11661, ai.n)
   return

* Out: ctx.file, the file the tree or the editor is on
get.context:
   ctx.file = ''
   if focus.id = 'tree' or nbuf = 0 then
      screen get f, 'tree' returning code
      if code[1, 2] = 'F:' then ctx.file = code[3, 99]
      if code[1, 2] = 'R:' then ctx.file = field(code, ':', 2)
   end
   if ctx.file = '' and nbuf > 0 then ctx.file = bn<cb>
   return

* Ctrl-W, as SED's: the text in the editor is written to a record of the file under another name and the editor goes on
* with the record it had
do.writeas:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11218)
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   u = bu<cb>
   k = 't' : u
   wa.text = bx{k}
   wa.file = bn<cb>
   screen ask sysmsg(11208) returning wa.id else return
   wa.id = trim(wa.id)
   if wa.id = '' then return
   open wa.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(11200, wa.file)
      return
   end
   gosub edit.check                  ;* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64
   if not(edit.ok) then return
   exists = @false
   read x from fv, wa.id then exists = @true
   if exists then
      msg = sysmsg(11217, wa.id, wa.file)
      return
   end
   write wa.text on fv, wa.id
   lp = 0
   nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
   if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
   for q = 1 to nl
      if lfiles<q> = wa.file then
         lp = q
         exit
      end
   next q
   if lp then
      lf.name = wa.file
      gosub load.file
      gosub tree.apply
      gosub redraw
   end
   msg = sysmsg(11202, wa.file, wa.id)
   return

* Ctrl-R, as SED's: a text is replaced by another all through the record in the editor (what undo remembered is lost:
* the text area is given the new text)
do.replace:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11218)
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(11232) returning rp.old else return
   if rp.old = '' then return
   screen ask sysmsg(11233) returning rp.new else return
   gosub stash.current
   u = bu<cb>
   k = 't' : u
   rp.text = bx{k}
   rp.n = count(rp.text, rp.old)
   if rp.n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(11234, 0)
      return
   end
   bx{k} = change(rp.text, rp.old, rp.new)
   gosub load.current
   msg = sysmsg(11234, rp.n)
   return

do.new:
   gosub get.context
   if ctx.file = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(11212)
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(11208) returning nm else return
   nm = trim(nm)
   if nm = '' then return
   open ctx.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(11200, ctx.file)
      return
   end
   gosub edit.check                  ;* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64
   if not(edit.ok) then return
   exists = @false
   read x from fv, nm then exists = @true
   if exists then
      msg = sysmsg(11217, nm, ctx.file)
      return
   end
   write '' on fv, nm
   lp = 0
   nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
   if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
   for q = 1 to nl
      if lfiles<q> = ctx.file then
         lp = q
         exit
      end
   next q
   if lp then
      lf.name = ctx.file
      gosub load.file
   end
   cur.code = 'R:' : ctx.file : ':' : nm
   or.file = ctx.file
   or.id = nm
   gosub open.record
   gosub tree.apply
   gosub redraw.tree
   if nbuf > 0 then
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
   end
   return

do.find:
   gosub get.context
   if ctx.file = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(11212)
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(11210, ctx.file) returning pat else return
   if pat = '' then return
   open ctx.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(11200, ctx.file)
      return
   end
   sselect fv to 9
   readlist ids from 9 else ids = ''
   n = dcount(ids, @fm)
   if ids = '' then n = 0
   if n > MAXREC then n = MAXREC
   upat = upcase(pat)
   hits = 0
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   sid = ''
   sln = ''
   for i = 1 to n
      read r from fv, ids<i> then
         nl = dcount(r, @fm)
         for j = 1 to nl
            if index(upcase(r<j>), upat, 1) then
               hits += 1
               err.rows<-1> = j : @vm : ids<i> : ': ' : trim(r<j>)
               err.lines<-1> = j
               sid<-1> = ids<i>
               sln<-1> = j
            end
         until hits >= MAXHIT
         next j
      end
   until hits >= MAXHIT
   next i
   out.kind = 'search'
   out.file = ctx.file
   gosub err.load
   msg = sysmsg(11211, hits, ctx.file)
   return

